00:00:04.470 --> 00:00:06.690 align:start position:0%
Pulitzer prize-winning historian Doris  Kearns Goodwin has researched American  presidents for a half-century she got  her big break back as a 24 year old  White House Fellow for a President  Lyndon Johnson and went on to help write  his memoirs over her career she's  written extensively about the lives of  presidents Abraham Lincoln Theodore  Roosevelt and Franklin Roosevelt in her  new online masterclass course she  teaches how leadership qualities can  define a commander-in-chief and she  joins us good morning glad to be with  you thank you so I want to get into the  master class but before I do that I want  a little dose of perspective here  historians always talk about how as  troubled as are times today may seem and  by many measures they are very troubled  indeed the past was worse please tell me  how yeah there was an old woman on the  plane who said to me tell me you're in  historian where times are really worse  so I started talking about the 1850s for  worse we had this partisan press you  only believed what newspaper you were  reading the Republican the democratic or  the Whig and that she said but that  didn't end up - well it was the Civil  War so I said right right but in truth  it is true I mean what think about  Lincoln coming in and the Civil War is  about to begin the Great Depression  World War two in the early days  somehow democracy gets through these  things and we have to believe that and  one of the ways in which you say in the  master class that democracy can come  through is if we make sure that we know  each other and one of the ways to solve  that in the present day would be a  volunteer program why do you think  that's so important I feel so strongly  if I could do one thing you know like in  my 50 years of being in this kind of  life of us an historian it would be to  have a national service program just  thing Teddy Roosevelt warns that the  reason democracy will fail is if people  in the other part of the country think  of themselves as the other different  sections if you're in a national service  program you're not going to the Peace  Corps abroad necessarily you're going  from the city to the country you're  hosted by a family you're learning how  to deal with national disasters you're  helping mentoring people you get time  off from college or for college tuition  you would just know what military people  get when my son joined the army right  after 9/11 nothing will equal that he  had just graduated Harvard he'd learned  to be in a platoon with all sorts of  people it'd be great for us to  understand each other again that you  become invested in the country you know  in a different way civil in in civics  and civic  we should be teaching it in school and  we should be learning it in such ways  like this what are the characteristics  of an effective leader would you say I  think there are a bunch of them I think  there's actually a family resemblance  from my guys that I studied over these  50 years I talk about them so humility  is one yes the ability to me that's an  interesting you you acknowledge your  errors you learn from your mistakes no  empathy is so important to be able to  see other people's points of view  resilience to get through adversity the  ability to create a team where people  can argue with you where you can share  credit and you can shoulder blame and I  think most importantly is the ambition  for self becomes an ambition for  something larger in the transformative  leaders so humility acknowledging errors  when you mentioned that in the context  of past presidents you think of the  current president who famously does not  say I'm sorry does not acknowledge  errors is not known for his humility is  he is he casting a new mould of  presidential leadership or what I don't  know it seems to be the most important  thing with all those qualities is the  ability to grow if you have humility and  empathy you can learn you can learn in  office you're not going to change  fundamentally once you become president  but you're hoping that leaders grow and  it's hard to grow if you can't  acknowledge hours because then you're  never wrong so what are you going to  learn from have you gotten a chance to  speak to president Truong no I love to I  mean I've spoken to many of the other  presidents and what would you say to him  I think what I'd say to him is can I  bring Lincoln and Teddy Roosevelt and  Franklin Roosevelt and LBJ in to talk to  you tonight I'd rather they be able to  talk to him than me but I'll channel  them and you listen to them and they'll  teach you something one story you tell  is about President Lincoln Lincoln  writing what you call hot letters to his  staff what exactly so he'd get mad at  somebody right and instead of yelling  and screaming at them he would write a  letter in which he expressed all of his  anger to general Meade after he failed  failed to follow up with General Lee's  army I'm immeasurably distressed you  didn't do what I asked you to do the  war's gonna go on forever now and then  he put the letter aside hoping he would  cool down psychologically and never send  it so we never even saw these until the  20th century when suddenly you see a  whole bunch of letters to people and the  interesting thing is I was talking to  President Obama about this when I did an  exit interview I said do you have  think of doing that said what do you  mean I said well do you do that I do it  all the time I said what do you mean he  writes letters and then throws them in  the basket and they never went anywhere  it's like the draft folder of one's  tweets exactly  cold tweets rather than hot tweets all  right great story Doris Kearns Goodwin  thank you so much her new master class  is available online now
 

